% ============================================================================
% fossil-preservation-subtype.query.adj — a worked recall over the geology
% fossil-preservation-subtype library.
% ============================================================================
% The shape a learner (or an LLM) produces to ANSWER "what kind of cast is a
% steinkern?": it states no earth-science fact of its own — it only
% `import`s the grounded table and asks binding queries. The engine resolves
% each `?` against the `fossil_preservation_subtype` rows and returns the
% parent type + description + the table's source/locator/trust. The second
% query runs the relation BACKWARD (bind the parent type `cast`, recall
% which sub-category belongs to it — steinkern). A peer-level type from the
% parent table (`mold`) is not itself a sub-category, so a recall on it
% abstains honestly — the engine never invents a sub-category relationship.
%
% Run (from this directory so the relative import resolves):
%     ../../../../packages/rust/target/debug/adj-lang-cli fossil-preservation-subtype.query.adj
% ============================================================================

import "fossil-preservation-subtype.adj"

? fossil_preservation_subtype(steinkern, $ParentType, $Description)  % expect cast, ...
? fossil_preservation_subtype($Subtype, cast, $Description)          % reverse bind — expect steinkern
? fossil_preservation_subtype(mold, $ParentType, $Description)       % expect abstain — mold is a peer type, not a subtype
